+// DCS VR Control (0004): the release delay steered on slack rather than on a
+// yes/no lateness test, for runtimes that pace the presenter with their own
+// wait (everything but SteamVR).
+//
+// Why: judged yes/no at the first synthetic's own hand-over, a first live
+// flight on Pimax OpenXR (DCS World, 90 Hz, 3X) logged "delay 0, 30 of 30
+// late, 30 unwritten, ceiling 22.2 ms" every second for the whole flight.
+// Its frames were queued ~1.5 ms before their first synthetic's slot and
+// that synthetic was written ~30 ms later: the delay could only ever be at
+// zero, which is right, but the test could not tell that from a title with
+// 28 ms of room and two slow frames a second - with any lateness at zero
+// delay it never raised, and it never said how much room there was.
+//
+// One sample per application frame: how long before its hand-over the
+// frame's first synthetic was written (negative: after it; a slot that came
+// with nothing queued is reported as minus one period). Judged kWindowFrames
+// at a time on the second-lowest sample, so one outlier a second neither
+// raises nor lowers it:
+//   low >= kSlackMargin   raise by what is left above the margin, at most
+//                         kMaxSlackRaise, through the same ceiling and hold
+//                         as observe_triple_release
+//   low < 0, delay > 0    back off by the shortfall plus the margin (at least
+//                         kBackoffStep), put the ceiling there and hold it,
+//                         doubling the hold for every further failure
+//   otherwise             in band, or late with nothing to give back: hold.
+// `late` in the window report counts negative samples.
+inline constexpr Nanoseconds kSlackMargin = 2 * kMillisecond;
+inline constexpr Nanoseconds kMaxSlackRaise = 4 * kMillisecond;
+
+TripleReleaseWindow observe_triple_slack(
+    TripleReleaseTuner& tuner, Nanoseconds period, Nanoseconds slack) noexcept;
